<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Opt-in real-hardware test parameters (see CLAUDE.md's "Testing" section). Not used unless a test
  run explicitly passes this file via dotnet test's "settings" option followed by this file's path
  (the literal flag spelling isn't written here because XML comments can't contain two consecutive
  hyphens, which that flag needs).

  Tests that read these (RealHardware*Tests classes) report Assert.Inconclusive - not a failure -
  when run without this file, so the default `dotnet test` (no settings flag) stays hardware-free.
  These are the same real devices already referenced by src/DevTerm.Console/Properties/launchSettings.json's
  "TCP 192.168.0.107:23 ASCII" / "TCP 192.168.0.108:23 ASCII" profiles (a real Tektronix 2230
  oscilloscope, reachable over a serial-to-Ethernet bridge) - override the values below (or pass
  your own settings file) if those two hosts aren't reachable from wherever this runs.

  RealTcpDeviceHost3 is a real Tektronix TDS2024 oscilloscope, also over a serial-to-Ethernet
  bridge - a different, SCPI-based device family: it identifies via "*IDN?" (SCPI standard), not
  the "ID?" the pre-SCPI 2230s above use.

  The *Query and *ExpectedReply values below are each host's identity-query text and expected reply
  substring, read by RealHardwareCliTests as separate parameters (not baked into the test as
  literals) so a new real device can be added later with only new parameters plus one new DataRow,
  no code change - and so an unverified guess can be corrected here instead of in code.
  RealTcpDeviceHost3ExpectedReply is now the full real-hardware-confirmed reply
  (docs/test/2026-09-25-15-02-44.md: "TEKTRONIX,TDS 2024,0,CF:91.1CT FV:v4.12 TDS2CM:CMV:v1.04") -
  that confirmation had landed in that session's report but was never propagated here until
  docs/test/2026-09-25-18-57-22.md caught the gap; tighten further only if a shorter/more specific
  substring is ever needed.

  Real Serial devices (RealHardwareSerialTests, in DevTerm.Console.Tests) - three bench SCPI power
  supplies/meters, each on its own COM port (COM ports are reassigned by Windows whenever a USB
  adapter is replugged into a different physical port, so these are per-machine parameters, not
  literals - the whole point of parameterizing them per the user's request). Each *ExpectedReply
  value is optional: leave it blank to skip the substring assertion and just require a non-null,
  non-timed-out reply to each query (the "pass as long as it doesn't fault or timeout" bar) -
  RealSerialKa6003pExpectedReply and RealSerialKa3005pExpectedIdnReply are filled in below because
  both *IDN? replies are real-hardware confirmed (docs/changes/2026-09-23.md for the KA6003P;
  docs/test/2026-09-25-15-02-44.md and docs/test/2026-09-25-18-57-22.md for the KA3005P); the HP
  34401A is left blank rather than asserting an unverified guess.

  Real USBTMC devices (RealHardwareUsbtmcTests, in DevTerm.Console.Tests) - four Rigol bench
  instruments. VendorId/ProductId are decimal (Windows/libusb report hex; convert before setting
  these, same convention as UsbtmcTransportOptions itself) and come from a real bench
  `- -listusbtmcdevices true` enumeration, not guesses - see docs/test/2026-09-24-07-16-34.md.
  0x1AB1 (Rigol Technologies) = 6833 decimal in every row below.

  RealUsbtmcDs1102eSerialNumber/RealUsbtmcDg1022SerialNumber matter here in a way none of the other
  real-hardware parameters do: the DS1102E and DG1022 enumerate under the exact same VID:PID
  (0x1AB1:0x0588 = 6833:1416), so VendorId/ProductId alone can't tell them apart -
  RealUsbtmcDs1102eSerialNumber is the DS1102E's own real, confirmed serial number
  ("DS1ET180300759", see rigol-ds1102e.json's Notes) and RealHardwareUsbtmcTests only matches a
  device whose serial number equals it exactly; RealUsbtmcDg1022SerialNumber is likewise the
  DG1022's own real, confirmed serial number (see below). The DG1062Z/DM3058E
  don't have a similar collision on this bench (nothing else shares their PID), so they don't
  need a SerialNumber to disambiguate - both are filled in anyway, with real, confirmed values.

  RealUsbtmcDs1102eExpectedIdnReply is filled in because the DS1102E's *IDN? reply is real-hardware
  confirmed (docs/changes/2026-09-24.md: "RIGOL TECHNOLOGIES,DS1102E,DS1ET180300759,00.04.02.01.00").
  RealUsbtmcDg1062zExpectedIdnReply is filled in too, for the same reason
  (docs/test/2026-09-25-15-02-44.md: "Rigol Technologies,DG1062Z,DG1ZA232603118,03.01.12") - its
  first attempts hit a USBTMC header-decode failure, since root-caused as a data-toggle desync from
  clearing endpoint halts on open (docs/test/2026-09-25-18-03-06.md) and fixed. The
  DM3058E row is filled in the same way (same report: "Rigol Technologies,DM3058E,DM3R232301438,
  01.01.00.02.03.01"); its old parked bulk-IN stall no longer reproduces.
-->
<RunSettings>
    <TestRunParameters>
        <Parameter name="RealTcpDeviceHost1" value="192.168.0.107" />
        <Parameter name="RealTcpDeviceHost2" value="192.168.0.108" />
        <Parameter name="RealTcpDeviceHost3" value="192.168.0.110" />
        <Parameter name="RealTcpDevicePort" value="23" />
        <Parameter name="RealTcpDeviceHost1Query" value="ID?" />
        <Parameter name="RealTcpDeviceHost2Query" value="ID?" />
        <Parameter name="RealTcpDeviceHost3Query" value="*IDN?" />
        <Parameter name="RealTcpDeviceHost1ExpectedReply" value="TEK/2230" />
        <Parameter name="RealTcpDeviceHost2ExpectedReply" value="TEK/2230" />
        <Parameter name="RealTcpDeviceHost3ExpectedReply" value="TEKTRONIX,TDS 2024" />

        <!-- HP/Agilent/Keysight 34401A: 9600 8N2, no handshake, LF terminator, real hardware confirmed
             (see docs/changes/2026-09-23.md). SYSTem:REMote must be sent before any query. -->
        <Parameter name="RealSerialHp34401aPort" value="COM9" />
        <Parameter name="RealSerialHp34401aBaud" value="9600" />
        <Parameter name="RealSerialHp34401aDataBits" value="8" />
        <Parameter name="RealSerialHp34401aParity" value="None" />
        <Parameter name="RealSerialHp34401aStopBits" value="Two" />
        <Parameter name="RealSerialHp34401aHandshake" value="None" />
        <Parameter name="RealSerialHp34401aExpectedIdnReply" value="" />

        <!-- Korad KA3005P: 9600 8N1, no handshake, no terminator (empty Terminator in its profile). -->
        <Parameter name="RealSerialKa3005pPort" value="COM6" />
        <Parameter name="RealSerialKa3005pBaud" value="9600" />
        <Parameter name="RealSerialKa3005pDataBits" value="8" />
        <Parameter name="RealSerialKa3005pParity" value="None" />
        <Parameter name="RealSerialKa3005pStopBits" value="One" />
        <Parameter name="RealSerialKa3005pHandshake" value="None" />
        <Parameter name="RealSerialKa3005pExpectedIdnReply" value="KORAD KA3005P" />

        <!-- Korad KA6003P: same settings as the KA3005P above. *IDN? reply confirmed against real
             hardware (docs/changes/2026-09-23.md): "KORAD KA6003P V5.8 SN:50266913" - the serial
             number is unit-specific, so only the model prefix is asserted. -->
        <Parameter name="RealSerialKa6003pPort" value="COM7" />
        <Parameter name="RealSerialKa6003pBaud" value="9600" />
        <Parameter name="RealSerialKa6003pDataBits" value="8" />
        <Parameter name="RealSerialKa6003pParity" value="None" />
        <Parameter name="RealSerialKa6003pStopBits" value="One" />
        <Parameter name="RealSerialKa6003pHandshake" value="None" />
        <Parameter name="RealSerialKa6003pExpectedIdnReply" value="KORAD KA6003P" />

        <!-- Real HID devices (RealHardwareK8055Tests/RealHardwareBusylightTests) - identified by
             DevicePath (preferred, per the user's request) with VendorId/ProductId as the fallback
             match when DevicePath is blank, mirroring SystemHidDevice.Open's own match order. -->
        <Parameter name="RealHidK8055-1-VendorId" value="4303" />
        <Parameter name="RealHidK8055-1-ProductId" value="21760" />
        <Parameter name="RealHidK8055-1-DevicePath" value="" />
        <Parameter name="RealHidK8055-2-VendorId" value="4303" />
        <Parameter name="RealHidK8055-2-ProductId" value="21761" />
        <Parameter name="RealHidK8055-2-DevicePath" value="" />
        <Parameter name="RealHidK8055-3-VendorId" value="4303" />
        <Parameter name="RealHidK8055-3-ProductId" value="21762" />
        <Parameter name="RealHidK8055-3-DevicePath" value="" />
        <Parameter name="RealHidK8055-4-VendorId" value="4303" />
        <Parameter name="RealHidK8055-4-ProductId" value="21763" />
        <Parameter name="RealHidK8055-4-DevicePath" value="" />
        <Parameter name="RealHidBusylightVendorId" value="1240" />
        <Parameter name="RealHidBusylightProductId" value="63560" />
        <Parameter name="RealHidBusylightDevicePath" value="" />

        <!-- Radex One (RealHardwareRadexOneTests) - VendorId/ProductId are still unconfirmed: a live
             HID enumeration pass on 2026-09-25 found no Radex One device attached (only an unrelated
             MSI "MYSTIC LIGHT" RGB controller). Left blank; the test reports Assert.Inconclusive
             until these are filled in with real values from a `--listhiddevices true` run against
             the actual device. See docs/changes/2026-09-25.md and TODO.md. -->
        <Parameter name="RealHidRadexOneVendorId" value="" />
        <Parameter name="RealHidRadexOneProductId" value="" />
        <Parameter name="RealHidRadexOneDevicePath" value="" />

        <!-- Rigol DM3058E multimeter: USBTMC, VID 0x1AB1 PID 0x09C4. SerialNumber/ExpectedIdnReply are
             real-hardware confirmed (docs/test/2026-09-25-18-03-06.md). -->
        <Parameter name="RealUsbtmcDm3058eVendorId" value="6833" />
        <Parameter name="RealUsbtmcDm3058eProductId" value="2500" />
        <Parameter name="RealUsbtmcDm3058eSerialNumber" value="DM3R232301438" />
        <Parameter name="RealUsbtmcDm3058eExpectedIdnReply" value="DM3058E" />

        <!-- Rigol DS1102E oscilloscope: USBTMC, VID 0x1AB1 PID 0x0588 - shares this exact VID:PID
             with a Rigol DG1022 also on this bench, so SerialNumber is required to target the
             DS1102E specifically (see the comment block above). -->
        <Parameter name="RealUsbtmcDs1102eVendorId" value="6833" />
        <Parameter name="RealUsbtmcDs1102eProductId" value="1416" />
        <Parameter name="RealUsbtmcDs1102eSerialNumber" value="DS1ET180300759" />
        <Parameter name="RealUsbtmcDs1102eExpectedIdnReply" value="DS1102E" />

        <!-- Rigol DG1062Z function generator: USBTMC, VID 0x1AB1 PID 0x0642. SerialNumber
             ("DG1ZA232603118") and ExpectedIdnReply ("DG1062Z") are real-hardware confirmed
             (docs/test/2026-09-25-15-02-44.md's second follow-up): *IDN? ->
             "Rigol Technologies,DG1062Z,DG1ZA232603118,03.01.12". SerialNumber isn't required to
             disambiguate this device (nothing else on this bench shares its PID) but is filled in
             here since it's now a confirmed real value. -->
        <Parameter name="RealUsbtmcDg1062zVendorId" value="6833" />
        <Parameter name="RealUsbtmcDg1062zProductId" value="1602" />
        <Parameter name="RealUsbtmcDg1062zSerialNumber" value="DG1ZA232603118" />
        <Parameter name="RealUsbtmcDg1062zExpectedIdnReply" value="DG1062Z" />

        <!-- Rigol DG1022 function generator: USBTMC, VID 0x1AB1 PID 0x0588 - shares this exact
             VID:PID with the DS1102E above, so SerialNumber is required to target the DG1022
             specifically (see the comment block above and RealHardwareUsbtmcTests's class doc
             comment). SerialNumber ("DG1D125306284") is real-hardware confirmed via
             `- -listusbtmcdevices true` (docs/test/2026-09-24-17-39-35.md, re-confirmed in
             docs/test/2026-09-25-18-03-06.md). ExpectedIdnReply ("DG1022") is real-hardware
             confirmed too, now that its USBTMC "no reply" failures are fixed (same 2026-09-25 report):
             *IDN? -> "RIGOL TECHNOLOGIES,DG1022 ,DG1D125306284,,00.02.00.06.00.02.07". -->
        <Parameter name="RealUsbtmcDg1022VendorId" value="6833" />
        <Parameter name="RealUsbtmcDg1022ProductId" value="1416" />
        <Parameter name="RealUsbtmcDg1022SerialNumber" value="DG1D125306284" />
        <Parameter name="RealUsbtmcDg1022ExpectedIdnReply" value="DG1022" />
    </TestRunParameters>

    <!-- Controls parallelism at the vstest.console.exe level (across test containers/assemblies) -->
    <RunConfiguration>
        <MaxCpuCount>0</MaxCpuCount>
        <!-- 0 = use all available cores; or set a specific number like 4 -->
    </RunConfiguration>

    <!-- Controls parallelism within MSTest (across classes/methods in the same assembly) -->
    <MSTest>
        <!-- This is a global per-test-method deadline enforced by the MSTest framework itself,
             separate from (and overriding) any per-step WaitAsync timeout written into a test body.
             Real-hardware tests that send several sequential commands (e.g. RealHardwareTcpTests's
             TDS2024 test: *IDN?, CH1?, TRIGger:MAIn:FREQuency?) can legitimately take longer than a
             short deadline to complete real round-trips - confirmed real-hardware-confirmed
             (docs/test/2026-09-25-18-57-22.md): the TDS2024 test answered *IDN?/CH1? correctly but
             was killed by this timeout mid-flight on the third query ("timed out after 5000ms"),
             not because the device failed to answer. Left generous rather than tight. -->
        <TestTimeout>60000</TestTimeout>
        <Parallelize>
            <Workers>0</Workers>
            <!-- 0 = number of available cores -->
            <Scope>MethodLevel</Scope>
            <!-- or ClassLevel -->
        </Parallelize>
    </MSTest>
</RunSettings>
